The bug is integer overflow. The multiplication `result *= i;` is done on a `u64`. `20!` fits in `u64`, but `21!` already exceeds `u64::MAX`, so the value starts wrapping before `25!` is finished. In release builds, Rust disables integer overflow checks by default, so the wrap is silent.

With default debug settings, this overflow would actually panic rather than print a correct value; if debug “worked”, overflow checks were likely disabled.

The fix is to use a wider type and checked arithmetic so `25!` is returned correctly and further overflow fails loudly instead of silently wrapping.

<solution language="rust" verdict="fix">
fn factorial(n: u64) -> u128 {
    let mut result: u128 = 1;
    for i in 1..=n {
        result = result.checked_mul(i as u128).expect("factorial overflow");
    }
    result
}

fn main() {
    println!("{}", factorial(20));
    println!("{}", factorial(25));
}
