message.content:
The bug is in these lines:

```go
positive := nums[:0]
negative := nums[:0]
```

Both `positive` and `negative` are made from the same input slice, so they share the same backing array. When you `append` to either one, Go writes directly into that shared array. Because both slices start with length `0`, their appends both begin at index `0`, so one slice’s appends can overwrite the other slice’s values. It also mutates the original `nums` slice.

Use separate slices so each append has its own backing array:

<solution language="go" verdict="fix">
package main

func filterPositiveAndNegative(nums []int) ([]int, []int) {
    var positive, negative []int

    for _, n := range nums {
        if n > 0 {
            positive = append(positive, n)
        } else if n < 0 {
            negative = append(negative, n)
        }
    }

    return positive, negative
}
</solution>

