There are two issues:

1. The comparison `data[i] == max_val` is wrong. `max_val` is meant to be the value with the longest streak so far, not the value of the current streak. So when the current streak is a different value, its count never grows correctly.
2. The maximum is only updated when a streak is interrupted by a different value. If the longest streak ends at the end of the vector, it is never compared against `max_count` after the loop.

For `[2, 2, 1, 1, 1]`, the `1` streak is never counted as length `3` because the code keeps comparing against the previous best value `2`, and the final `1` streak is never evaluated after the loop ends.

<solution language="rust" verdict="fix">
fn longest_streak(data: &Vec<i32>) -> (i32, usize) {
    let mut max_val = data[0];
    let mut max_count: usize = 1;
    let mut current_count: usize = 1;

    for i in 1..data.len() {
        if data[i] == data[i - 1] {
            current_count += 1;
        } else {
            if current_count > max_count {
                max_count = current_count;
                max_val = data[i - 1];
            }
            current_count = 1;
        }
    }

    if current_count > max_count {
        max_count = current_count;
        max_val = data[data.len() - 1];
    }

    (max_val, max_count)
}
</solution>

